KNOWNBUG
dual_write_port1.sv
--bound 0
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^file .* line .*: `Verilog::\$root\.main\.mem' has multiple drivers$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
verilog_rtl_buildert::commit (src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp) tracks, per
identifier, the bit-slices written by every always block and rejects any
overlap as a driver conflict. This is correct for ordinary registers, but
a memory array with more than one write port is legitimate synthesizable
hardware: RTL construction has no notion of a memory array with several
write ports, and hence cannot model true dual (or multi) write-port RAMs.
